{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789303369,"nonce":"0zherwLmNGt/IykYOzUZb62bxrs=","title":"Linux: a native helper that raises via hlp_error loses the compiled callers","message":"The Linux x86-64 throw walk (`hlp_capture_exception_stack_from_frame`,\nreached through `ash_throw_stack_boundary`) names the innermost compiled\npc from glibc `backtrace()` and then follows the rbp chain for the\ncallers. That assumes rbp still holds the thrower's frame pointer when\nthe walk starts. Rust omits frame pointers on x86_64 Linux, so a runtime\nhelper that raises through `hlp_error` (not directly through `hlp_throw`\nfrom compiled code) may have used rbp as an ordinary register, and the\nchain is cut at the first frame: the trace has the innermost frame and\nnothing above it.\n\nTwo shapes reproduce it on the NUC, both with `--mode jit`:\n\n    var d:Dynamic = {}; d.nope = 1;        // hlp_obj_lookup_set -\u003e hlp_error\n    var p:{x:Int} = d;                      // hl_to_virtual -\u003e hlp_error\n\nCallers are missing on Linux; macOS (frame pointers kept by the ABI)\nprints the full chain.\n\nFix options: read rbp from the unwinder at the first non-native frame\n(`_Unwind_GetGR(ctx, 6)` inside `_Unwind_Backtrace`, which restores\ncallee-saved registers per frame) and start the rbp chain there; or build\nash_std with `-C force-frame-pointers=yes` on Linux, which also makes the\ndouble-visit dedupe (`return_pc != innermost_pc`) unnecessary.\n\nRelated: the interp/hybrid arms abort with \"hlp_throw called without\nactive trap\" on the `d.nope = 1` shape, a separate defect.","files":null}]}